[{:db/id "q-156596/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b7a1f339-0f7b-5c41-a416-123564f4177d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156596/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "24ea52ee-75fa-5a69-bf52-61c8054fc17c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156596/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "04e2c369-611e-582c-b3b2-75a08d2d3d59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156596/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "bfca8582-0761-507c-94f2-74ad23765611" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156596/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "01a16997-cfe1-598c-b310-4c60a767d862" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156596/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f178e38f-2fd9-5286-9dea-7cccd6f4bd40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-156596/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer-field/choices ["q-156596/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-156596/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-156596/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-156596/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-156596/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156596" :question/id #uuid "f04866c2-5821-588e-8c6b-02e6d3b6d9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-156596" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=-x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $y=-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\ (0) & =-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-156596/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156596"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-156624/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "eeb82d77-189b-5aea-8283-cfa724bbb7af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156624/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" :answer/id #uuid "fa758189-db7b-537d-a214-ccc51c377ffe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156624/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "190d764e-aadf-5baf-bee6-ec99c6fe2de3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156624/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "4a75a62f-b160-581a-a262-dd61173df2c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156624/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d440b781-4f01-5687-a703-a55f9fd29dd1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156624/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "73b1c4b7-e883-53e1-b69e-4e1fea18079b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-156624/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-156624/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-156624/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" "q-156624/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-156624/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-156624/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156624" :question/id #uuid "a59ba5af-9832-5f1d-ba3c-a65b6aec7513" :question/math-academy-id "q-156624" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=2y-1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x=2y-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(1)-1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-156624/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156624"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157290/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "64568db7-cb92-571d-b30f-5bc663d313de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157290/selection/0aba8feb8e80d6993512d064e058711be97ecf2a5cf7dd862ab928425f31c38c" :answer/id #uuid "6465787e-3b57-5d47-93cb-05f969206c7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157290/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "425c3071-3886-599a-96e7-5d3783934806" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157290/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "11884d3a-a72f-5116-8fe1-27612feda244" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157290/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "30526b57-8886-5e9b-85bb-f493dc26ea48" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157290/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b49e240-e720-5f28-b066-c1115d63a18b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-157290/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer-field/choices ["q-157290/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-157290/selection/0aba8feb8e80d6993512d064e058711be97ecf2a5cf7dd862ab928425f31c38c" "q-157290/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" "q-157290/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-157290/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157290" :question/id #uuid "c3687629-f6d5-54fc-ad8f-22bf8e69b542" :question/math-academy-id "q-157290" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2y=3x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $2y=3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-157290/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157290"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-156628/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a" :answer/id #uuid "581a7fc8-4873-5e92-a1b5-e48267fe4504" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156628/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "16b96773-cef1-5881-a20e-d88911b80e94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156628/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "011b7542-4ceb-5a6e-9194-1297db03933c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156628/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1a4c6cff-08e0-5962-a3fb-352535ac15e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156628/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "7e6ad365-c2c6-52a2-94f8-3765aaab8c7a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156628/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "08d13a0a-0538-5e53-ada9-3cdbfa592b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-156628/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer-field/choices ["q-156628/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a" "q-156628/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-156628/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-156628/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-156628/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156628" :question/id #uuid "99e53fa9-92f1-56cf-b70a-94abbb5cebc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-156628" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=5y$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $x=5y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5y \\\\ (0) & =5(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}5a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}5b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}5a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5a+5b \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $x=5y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}5a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5ka \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $x=5y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-156628/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156628"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-156653/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "7699a693-850c-5bbc-ba7f-e84391e12d0e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156653/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "e2708ba1-a181-5ec7-b891-ce7a83fd6359" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156653/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "510437b9-6232-5186-96e6-2d02ea5893c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156653/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "31bddf97-59f2-5c07-bfde-6ac965c19f39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156653/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "9dad1553-84d9-507c-8da4-6168af8de133" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156653/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "876351b5-b247-5edb-99d3-9c82a2391773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-156653/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer-field/choices ["q-156653/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-156653/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-156653/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-156653/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-156653/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156653" :question/id #uuid "0700e451-5c81-5537-b6d9-7820625a3801" :question/math-academy-id "q-156653" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=-3x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $y=-3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\ (0) & =-3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-3a)+(-3b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -3(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-3a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -3(ka)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-156653/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156653"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-155957"] :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2,2,-2)$ and $(3,-4,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{-6}=\\frac{z+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,-2,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(1)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(-2)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-156727/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a" :answer/id #uuid "280d8717-17ca-5875-84c1-5c13383701dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156727/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c61fd597-5eb4-5da1-9b18-3df4344a4f67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156727/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "3cb84162-37be-5dcb-9d87-b46a2b8a0167" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156727/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" :answer/id #uuid "b7816e7b-a719-5793-b0e8-6c58ea02da22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156727/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "4cf3594e-92ec-5d64-a9e6-9cac25a04db2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156727/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a6506c-7762-54ac-bb89-5cf016215df2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-156727/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-156727/selection/6578e6ba0baf24b357651e14e1811fc4e1af585d8babc3d67097617d0e105e7a" "q-156727/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-156727/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-156727/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" "q-156727/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156727" :question/id #uuid "a03a9055-1ec4-5305-b9e6-a5baebe071e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-156727" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,2,3)$ and $(3,2,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since the second coordinate of any point on this line satisfies\n\n$$\ny=2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-156727/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156727"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157375/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "acb01be4-7e6b-517f-8270-d2bed7e9c03c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157375/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "31dd66cc-3d3c-5437-94cb-7b5b526542e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157375/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8a72d94d-4de7-5660-b8d2-68397b134cea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157375/selection/0aba8feb8e80d6993512d064e058711be97ecf2a5cf7dd862ab928425f31c38c" :answer/id #uuid "6ba733d6-8ff5-5bdd-a892-a12b76600018" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157375/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "56ca87e5-84e3-5805-ba33-48b02c054cbf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157375/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb98135f-6b79-511a-af69-fa33343b0a77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-157375/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer-field/choices ["q-157375/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" "q-157375/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-157375/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-157375/selection/0aba8feb8e80d6993512d064e058711be97ecf2a5cf7dd862ab928425f31c38c" "q-157375/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157375" :question/id #uuid "353188f1-71a6-5ff8-b4bb-4d7890a4dd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157375" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,3,-2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4a+4b \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ -2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(4a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ k(-2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ -2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-157375/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157375"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157377/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "4ffe409b-9fe1-5e71-b667-0a961d5abc7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157377/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e2fbc6ef-82f2-5caf-a718-ba841e23c93c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157377/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" :answer/id #uuid "c3621910-713f-5952-a5a3-97cb53981be6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157377/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2396ecc9-d12a-57c8-9896-60466bf3f64c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157377/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "db4f2d19-32fd-5232-9965-9da309cd7e87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157377/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c616fa8-e625-5327-86ec-25b9c7bad100" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-157377/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer-field/choices ["q-157377/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-157377/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-157377/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" "q-157377/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-157377/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157377" :question/id #uuid "fed5d779-6797-53a6-928a-1ca17d13d811" :question/math-academy-id "q-157377" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,1,1)$ and $(2,-1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=z-1.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n(0)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (0)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(3,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n(3)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (3)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-157377/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157377"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157300/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6b3af7a7-97a3-5ffb-a288-176fdca56e1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157300/selection/0aba8feb8e80d6993512d064e058711be97ecf2a5cf7dd862ab928425f31c38c" :answer/id #uuid "2131350f-c569-5e35-9496-5430a8d749a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157300/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "6fd20dc2-aac6-521b-b64e-a221eb5ef8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157300/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "6b29f215-d33d-5b4d-b9c4-ace979e7debc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157300/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "db840929-0c6d-5786-aaf4-bb71e9415445" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157300/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ddc2f2d-6353-5c48-be48-c612a0233b7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-157300/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer-field/choices ["q-157300/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-157300/selection/0aba8feb8e80d6993512d064e058711be97ecf2a5cf7dd862ab928425f31c38c" "q-157300/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" "q-157300/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" "q-157300/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157300" :question/id #uuid "21c2846d-42ab-595b-825b-a71a1808f166" :question/math-academy-id "q-157300" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-157300/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157300"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157463/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "1fa4c8f2-42d9-57da-996f-be3795b379bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157463/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5e1d2b69-f372-5934-8206-763d3d78aad2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157463/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a3331b57-2794-5a34-8a46-e864ee15d9f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157463/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "a4fcb77a-b241-56f2-8b2a-6495dec0b8e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157463/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2abc3e12-1c84-5088-814e-2b55cab2fb9b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157463/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "53c5b6cc-2be2-51e2-98b3-6996390b7483" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-157463/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer-field/choices ["q-157463/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" "q-157463/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-157463/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-157463/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" "q-157463/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157463" :question/id #uuid "3ddd6e2c-f3ab-504b-85e4-6e7fa87c924a" :question/math-academy-id "q-157463" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2(k{a}_{1})-k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-157463/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157463"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-156728/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "a154f6cb-63a3-5e24-8bcd-3340f1e8c7b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156728/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" :answer/id #uuid "9b139c47-a395-556a-87fd-157d13003b8f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156728/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" :answer/id #uuid "adc0e550-32ce-5477-a222-1610d1282f15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156728/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "558ac58e-14af-5dfb-a6ac-773f14043a12" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156728/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "366325f2-8db5-5d8c-bf28-a813fccbf802" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156728/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "045c374a-2a1f-595f-bbd8-fce31044abbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-156728/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer-field/choices ["q-156728/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-156728/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" "q-156728/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" "q-156728/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" "q-156728/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156728" :question/id #uuid "b2fdea56-73c0-52eb-9e1b-09d0f326257e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156728" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-5y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-5y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-5y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-5y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-5(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-5({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2k{a}_{1}-5k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-156728/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156728"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-156685"] :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-157468/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "77c801ca-c8b5-544c-a29d-14e48f1d2bca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157468/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" :answer/id #uuid "0b07e8c3-4c8e-59c5-bfec-14c113f12088" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157468/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "b533c0af-2dbe-57bd-ac12-4207d4957760" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157468/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" :answer/id #uuid "27df7357-7605-5a94-b294-2aff462a1fb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157468/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "a7091a30-7cfd-5e7e-a388-650ae636a02e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157468/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc435495-3a7f-5173-8f2a-5ac136d628f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-157468/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-157468/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-157468/selection/b733e7a470ea14913327e50fae585d7231209b41e08de1ba47b5dbb3c5f7d7d6" "q-157468/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" "q-157468/selection/48ed73fa8fff01bdc324b4f21abd0bd25d38fe050f7a804787ee07e14c349dd0" "q-157468/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157468" :question/id #uuid "534f7159-4e37-511b-a177-3ef2f0afb878" :question/math-academy-id "q-157468" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x+y-2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (0)+(0)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (5)+(1)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-157468/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157468"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157464/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "b0878746-cd08-58ab-ba42-936ef2af0414" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157464/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "4360a05d-c83e-539f-bbc1-91f5b4d7f7df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157464/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "9863b51c-a10b-550a-aaba-5f9bc6f82bf9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157464/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" :answer/id #uuid "27ddeeae-c2ae-56f6-8b71-e9b071b37aee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157464/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "91f54be9-ff30-5745-993e-ef6fff518ad5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157464/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a88a5d9-3f05-56d8-8db5-90a8a56a81de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-157464/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer-field/choices ["q-157464/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-157464/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" "q-157464/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-157464/selection/a93f071c9e37df87ddd39fa5ca5c1b993bc7a9722336877075659d8a831d9910" "q-157464/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157464" :question/id #uuid "59b082ca-e2d3-50a9-9c3c-55f1318613c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157464" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+2y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+2(k{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-157464/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157464"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10974"] :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=5+3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/8a5ed52cd0a1f16d2284a101a757e57156f76aa24e11a1d15cbfacaa7e5c77b6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=5+3x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ (0) & =5+3(0) \\\\ 0 & =5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/9468a26323576c7ab92150c53c8cbc27cc719f91d096547f55d698643cf15658.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/513426187d03c0ee2d650b57432c6370e74aa81ec87cdfe92490aec2780aa9cb.png) from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+(-1) \\\\ 5+2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/38aed9478a8cb36691b5f05dbfe1fd0f9d8555ea43b94a21ee43e265d5a2afe1.png) since substituting the coordinates into the equation $y=5+3x$ gives the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ 7 & =5+3(-1) \\\\ 7 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10977"] :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(1,2,2)$ and $(2,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/10865a16bf90e96dc3d6fb4897751a84b0db7a12c55843c73767f77e8817bc0f.png)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n{x-1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n{(0)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}.  \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/f55d965d26e9f36702c88b259e7f60c84762894dccf7a72c9429fe84727fd922.png)\nHowever, the point $(3,3,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n{(3)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10980"] :question/problem "Let $\\Pi$ be the plane with equation $x+4y-3z=2$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\Pi$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- $\\Pi$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- $\\Pi$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $\\Pi$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/ff70a23af671918e56523ccd14c7c52bcafe502b3d5656f4e7a3f47606eda810.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (0)+4(0)-3(0) & =2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/d08091d57940e8ef91dc371a628eae1c1dc7395cf2d284f48915593116a59510.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/assets/3117536c9d0c6d9e228d909405e8542abdc50dfda5cef00e92719086d795855f.png) Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+1 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (3)+4(1)-3(1) & =2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
